  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: CareDisc 6
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "A cancer research team is exploring new treatments for pancreatic cancer.
    Before choosing what to study next, they meet with a community advisory board
    that includes cancer patients and caregivers. As a member of the board, you discuss
    how your mother\u2019s treatment caused severe nerve pain that made it hard to
    walk or hold objects. The team takes note of this and decides to explore new
    treatments that might avoid this side effect.\n\nWhy is it important for researchers
    to hear from caregivers at this early stage?"
  explanation: Caregivers see firsthand how cancer and its treatments affect daily
    life. Their experiences help scientists focus early research on what truly matters
    to patients and families. By including these perspectives from the start, researchers
    can design treatments that are not only effective, but also easier, safer, and
    more supportive for those living with cancer.
  correctAnswer: 'A: Because caregivers can help determine which treatments move
    forward to clinical trials'
  incorrectAnswer1: 'B: Because caregivers know how to design and test new treatments
    in the lab'
  incorrectAnswer2: 'C: Because caregivers can help researchers write medical regulations
    for new treatments once approved'
  incorrectAnswer3: 'D: Because caregivers can share what lesser-known side effects
    and challenges affect daily life most'
  questionRole: Caregiver
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Discovery
